"Chern's inner-series coefficient-positivity conjecture — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/356073/chern-s-inner-series-coefficient-positivity-conjecture)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Chern's inner-series coefficient-positivity conjecture ... The source presents this as a sufficient condition for the preceding double-series conjecture after rearranging the double sum, so it is a more general-looking auxiliary formulation of the same coefficient-positivity goal.\n\nChern's inner-series coefficient-positivity conjecture\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $m,s\\geq 1$m,s≥1 be integers. Consider the formal $q$q-series\n\n$$\\sum_{k\\geq 0}\\frac{q^k(1-q^k)}{(q^s;q^m)_k}.$$k≥0∑​(q s;q m)k​q k(1−q k)​.\n\nChern's conjecture. This $q$q-series has nonnegative coefficients in its expansion. The source presents this as a sufficient condition for the preceding double-series conjecture after rearranging the double sum, so it is a more general-looking auxiliary formulation of the same coefficient-positivity goal.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Damanvir Singh Binner, “On conjectures of Chern concerning parity bias in partitions”, arXiv:2209.05949 (2022).\n\ncoefficient-positivityq-series\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 5 days ago\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBarile–Macchia resolutions | Journal of Algebraic Combinatorics | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10801-023-01293-9)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; First, we provide an ideal whose Barile–Macchia, Taylor, and Lyubeznik resolutions and Scarf complex are all non-isomorphic and the only minimal one among these is a Barile–Macchia resolution (Theorem 5.3).\n\n# Barile–Macchia resolutions\n\n  * Open access\n  * Published: 05 February 2024\n\n  * Volume 59, pages 413–472 (2024)\n  * Cite this article\n\nYou have full access to this open access article\n\nDownload PDF\n\nSave article\n\nView saved research\n\nJournal of Algebraic Combinatorics Aims and scope Submit manuscript\n\nBarile–Macchia resolutions\n\nDownload PDF\n\n  * Trung Chau^{1} &\n  * Selvi Kara  ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-1464-2659^{2} \n\n  * 2027 Accesses\n\n  * 6 Citations\n\n  * 1 Altmetric\n\n  * Explore all metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe construct cellular resolutions for monomial ideals via discrete Morse theory. In particular, we develop an algorithm to create homogeneous acyclic matchings and we call the cellular resolutions induced from these matchings Barile–Macchia resolutions. Unfortunately, weighted oriented forests do not have this property in general (Example 4.17). We also show it is often possible to find a weighted oriented path (resp, cycle) from a given weighted oriented cycle (resp, path) such that the total Betti numbers of their edge ideals are equal (Theorem 4.18).\n\nIn a more general direction, we compare Barile–Macchia resolutions with Scarf complexes, Taylor, and Lyubeznik resolutions (see Sect. 5). First, we provide an ideal whose Barile–Macchia, Taylor, and Lyubeznik resolutions and Scarf complex are all non-isomorphic and the only minimal one among these is a Barile–Macchia resolution (Theorem 5.3). Next, we study when these resolutions or the Scarf complex coincide with a Barile–Macchia resolution. The most immediate case is the Taylor resolutions as they coincide with Barile–Macchia resolutions when the Barile–Macchia matching is the empty set. In the case of Lyubeznik resolutions, while there are examples where the two resolutions are not comparable in general, we identify sufficient conditions under which Barile–Macchia resolutions are closer to minimal ones (Theorem 5.14). In addition, we prove that Barile–Macchia resolutions coincide minimally with the Scarf complex under a natural assumption, recovering a result of Yuzvinsky [45, Proposition 4.4] (Theorem 5.4). Finally, we generalize Barile–Macchia resolutions in the same way Batzies and Welker generalize Lyubeznik resolutions in [4]. We briefly compare these two generalized versions (Theorem  5.19).\n\nOur paper is structured as follows: Sect. 2 contains the background regarding discrete Morse theory (Sect. 2.1), the Barile–Macchia algorithm (Sect. 2.2), Barile–Macchia matchings, and the notion of bridge-friendliness (Sect. 2.3). We present a criterion to check bridge-friendliness in Lemma 2.34 which proves to be quite useful in our work. In addition, we show that the corresponding Barile–Macchia resolution of a bridge-friendly ideal is minimal in Theorem 2.29. In Sect. 3, we focus on edge ideals of weighted oriented forests and prove Theorem 3.11. Edge ideals of weighted oriented cycles are studied in Sect. 4. In particular, we focus on independence of directions in Sect. 4.1, non-classic cycles in Sect. 4.2, and classic cycles in Sect. 4.3. In Sect. 5, we provide a comparison between Barile–Macchia resolutions and some well-known simplicial resolutions and complexes. In Sect. 6, we introduce some open questions and conjectures.\n\n### Proof\n\nLet \\(n=|\\sigma |\\), the cardinality of \\(\\sigma \\). We prove these statements using descending induction on n. If \\(n=|{{\\mathcal {G}}}(I)|\\), then \\(\\sigma = {{\\mathcal {G}}}(I)\\). Since any \\(m\\in {{\\mathcal {G}}}(I)\\) is in \\(\\sigma \\), it does not have any gaps. If \\(\\sigma \\) has a bridge, then \\((\\sigma , \\sigma {\\setminus } {{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\sigma )) \\in A\\) by Algorithm 2.9. Thus, \\(\\sigma \\) is type-2. Otherwise, \\(A= \\emptyset \\) and the Taylor resolution is minimal. Hence, the theorem holds for the base case \\(n=|{{\\mathcal {G}}}(I)|\\). Suppose the statement of the theorem holds for all subsets of \\({{\\mathcal {G}}}(I)\\) of cardinality \\(n+1\\).\n\n  1. (a)\n\nIf \\(\\sigma \\) is type-1, then there is a directed edge \\((\\tau , \\sigma ) \\in A\\) where \\(\\sigma = \\tau {\\setminus } {{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\tau )\\). It follows from Proposition 2.22 that \\({{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\tau )\\) is a true gap of \\(\\sigma \\) not dominating any bridges of \\(\\sigma \\). For the reverse implication, suppose \\(\\sigma \\) has a true gap which does not dominate any bridges of \\(\\sigma \\) and m is its smallest true gap. We first present the statement of each case and then obtain a contradiction based on the statement.\n\n  * The monomial \\(m_{zw}\\) is the only monomial in \\(\\sigma \\) that is in the same block as \\(m_{xy}, m_{xz}, m_{zw}\\) and is divisible by w. Since \\(m_{zw}\\) is a bridge of \\(\\sigma \\cup m_{xz}\\) but not a bridge of \\(\\sigma \\), each pair satisfying (a) for \\(m_{zw}\\) must involve \\(m_{xz}\\). Thus, there exists \\(wu \\in E({{\\mathcal {T}}})\\) such that \\(m_{wu}\\in \\sigma \\) and \\(m_{xz},m_{zw},m_{wu}\\) are in the same block. Recall that \\(m_{xy},m_{xz},m_{zw}\\) are all in the same block as \\(m_{xz}\\) is a gap of \\(\\sigma \\). Thus, \\(m_{xy},m_{xz},m_{zw},m_{wu}\\) form a potential block. Therefore, \\(m_{wu}\\) is in the same block as \\(m_{xy}, m_{xz}, m_{zw}\\) and it is divisible by w. This is in contradiction to the statement of this case.\n\n  * There exist \\( m_{zz'}, m_{ww'}\\in \\sigma \\) such that \\(m_{zz'}, m_{ww'}, m_{zw}\\) are in the same block where \\(zw\\ne zz', ww'\\). Then \\(m_{zw}\\) is a bridge of \\(\\sigma \\), a contradiction.\n\nSimilar arguments apply for the case \\(m=m_{xy}\\) where \\(z>y\\) using (ii) instead of (i). \\(\\square \\)\n\nIn particular, if a monomial m is a true gap of some subset \\(\\sigma \\) of \\({{\\mathcal {G}}}({{\\mathcal {T}}})\\), then m is still a true gap of \\(\\sigma \\cup m'\\) as long as m dominates \\(m'\\). Thus, the main theorem of this section follows immediately from Lemma 2.34.\n\n### Proof\n\nWe adopt the notations from Theorem 2.3. Our goal is to show that the differentials in the Morse resolution are exactly those of the corresponding subcomplex of the Taylor resolution. This is equivalent to show \\(\\sigma ''=\\sigma '\\) for each \\(\\sigma '\\) and \\(\\sigma ''\\) such that \\(\\sigma '\\subseteq \\sigma , |\\sigma '|=|\\sigma |-1\\), and \\(\\sigma ''\\) is a critical subset where \\(|\\sigma '|=|\\sigma ''|\\). For the sake of contradiction, suppose the set of critical subsets form a simplicial complex and \\(\\sigma ''\\ne \\sigma '\\) for such \\(\\sigma '\\) and \\(\\sigma ''\\). By our assumption, \\(\\sigma '\\) is  critical.\n\nConsider a gradient path \\(\\sigma '=\\sigma _1 \\rightarrow \\sigma _2 \\rightarrow \\ldots \\rightarrow \\sigma _k=\\sigma ''\\) from \\(\\sigma '\\) to \\(\\sigma ''\\). By the definition of gradient paths, we have either \\((\\sigma _i,\\sigma _{i+1}) \\notin A\\) or \\((\\sigma _{i+1},\\sigma _i) \\in A\\) for each \\(i\\in [k-1]\\). Observe that each directed edge \\((\\sigma _i,\\sigma _{i+1})\\) along this gradient path must be alternating between these two types of edges. If \\((\\sigma ',\\sigma _2) \\notin A\\), then \\((\\sigma '', \\sigma _{k-1}) \\in A\\) since \\(|\\sigma |=|\\sigma ''|\\). This means \\(\\sigma ''\\) is not a critical subset, a contradiction. If \\((\\sigma _2, \\sigma ') \\in A\\), then \\(\\sigma '\\) is not a critical subset, a contradiction. This completes the proof. \\(\\square \\)\n\nLyubeznik resolutions, the Taylor resolution, and the Scarf complex of a monomial ideal are not necessarily isomorphic. These three complexes may also differ from Barile–Macchia resolutions.\n\n### Theorem 5.3\n\nThere exists an ideal I such that its Taylor and Lyubeznik resolutions and Scarf complex are all non-isomorphic from one another, and non-isomorphic to one of its Barile–Macchia resolutions which is minimal.\n\n### Proof\n\nLet \\(I=(xy,yz,zw,wx)\\subseteq R:=k[x,y,z,w]\\). It turns out that all the Lyubeznik resolutions of R/I are