\({{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\sigma )>_I m_L(\\sigma )\\) under these assumptions.\n\n\\(\\square \\)\n\nNow we are ready to prove the main result of this section: A Barile–Macchia resolution is at most as big as the corresponding Lyubeznik resolution under certain hypotheses. In order to illustrate possible scenarios, we will use green and blue arrows to depict directed edges in \\(A_L\\) and \\(A_B\\), respectively. Additionally, we use blue dashed arrows to depict directed edges of the form \\((\\sigma ,\\sigma \\setminus {{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\sigma ))\\) where \\(\\sigma \\) is potentially type-2, but not type-2.\n\n### Theorem 5.14\n\nLet I be a monomial ideal. Assume there exist no two subsets \\(\\sigma \\) and \\(\\tau \\) of \\({{\\mathcal {G}}}(I)\\) such that \\(\\sigma {\\setminus } {{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\sigma )=\\tau {\\setminus } {{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\tau )\\), \\({{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\tau )>_I {{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\sigma )\\), \\(m_L(\\sigma )=m_L(\\tau )>_I {{\\,\\textrm{sb}\\,}}(\\tau )\\), and \\(m_L(\\tau )\\in \\tau \\). Then \\(V_L\\subseteq V_B\\). In particular, \\({{\\,\\textrm{rank}\\,}}({\\mathcal {F}}_L)_i\\ge {{\\,\\textrm{rank}\\,}}({\\mathcal {F}}_B)_i\\) for each \\(i\\in {\\mathbb {Z}}\\) where \\({\\mathcal {F}}_L\\) and \\({\\mathcal {F}}_B\\) are the induced Lyubeznik and Barile–Macchia resolutions, respectively.\n\n### Proof\n\nRecall that \\(m_L(\\sigma \\cup m_L(\\sigma ))=m_L(\\sigma )\\) by definition. Thus, it suffices to show that for each \\(\\tau \\in V_L\\) satisfying \\((\\tau ,\\tau \\setminus m_L(\\tau ))\\in A_L\\), both \\(\\tau \\) and \\(\\tau \\setminus m_L(\\tau )\\) appear in some edges of \\(A_B\\). To be more specific, these two subsets of \\({{\\mathcal {G}}}(I)\\) can appear in \\(A_B\\) in one of the following three  patterns:\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBarile–Macchia resolutions (https://d-nb.info/1333391951/34)\nciteturn1search12 [wo